WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:03.282
as I'm always watching.

00:00:03.282 --> 00:00:05.187
I think you've made a
really strong transition

00:00:05.187 --> 00:00:08.462
into life after basketball

00:00:08.462 --> 00:00:10.312
but before before we go into that

00:00:11.382 --> 00:00:12.522
actually this is a great question

00:00:12.522 --> 00:00:15.112
'cause I know a ton of my listeners

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:16.882
are gonna have a lot of awareness of you.

00:00:16.882 --> 00:00:18.602
So I think the right first question is

00:00:18.602 --> 00:00:20.322
what do you been up to?

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:23.112
- Man, that is such a good question.

00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:24.702
- [Gary] Yeah, let me give you the floor.

00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:25.815
- Yeah I've been,

00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:28.602
first of all kind of had to rebound

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:30.955
in my situation.

00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:34.272
- [Gary] How old are you right now?

00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:35.282
I'm 35.

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:37.622
So people really don't realize

00:00:37.622 --> 00:00:39.252
and I didn't realize either

00:00:39.252 --> 00:00:43.732
really what happens to an athlete
after you're done playing.

00:00:43.732 --> 00:00:45.642
We kind of just disappear and go away

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:48.312
and maybe you'll see us on TV again

00:00:48.312 --> 00:00:51.188
but that's when I think
real life stars for us.

00:00:51.188 --> 00:00:52.021
- [Gary] Yeah, I think that's right.

00:00:52.021 --> 00:00:53.192
- Yeah, I have five children.

00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:55.150
- [Gary] That's a great number.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.332
- Yeah, it's an awesome number, man

00:00:57.332 --> 00:00:59.882
and then getting into the swing of things

00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:02.722
into being a father that's there more,

00:01:02.722 --> 00:01:04.699
into being a husband that's there more.

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:05.532
- [Gary] And by the way, takes practice.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.365
- [Chris] Yeah it does.

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:08.205
- People don't understand that,

00:01:08.205 --> 00:01:11.052
as for me going hard
and then having children

00:01:11.052 --> 00:01:13.692
and even like evaluating if I'm
doing too much or too little

00:01:13.692 --> 00:01:15.052
or whether it's workout regimen

00:01:15.052 --> 00:01:18.892
or business like that is
a transition in itself.

00:01:18.892 --> 00:01:20.205
- [Chris] Yeah it becomes a balancing act.

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:21.362
- It's not just so all
of a sudden you're like

00:01:21.362 --> 00:01:22.272
going to daycare and,

00:01:22.272 --> 00:01:24.212
like it's a thing.

00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:27.452
- Yeah, yeah and school
and getting them prepared

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:29.152
and moving back and forth

00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:30.882
and moving my family to Austin.

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:33.232
- And your situation's a little bit unique

00:01:33.232 --> 00:01:35.792
in the fact that obviously

00:01:35.792 --> 00:01:38.732
'cause I follow sports
enough to know like it was,

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:40.392
I don't think as many
people will know this,

00:01:40.392 --> 00:01:42.142
it wasn't the easiest situation

00:01:42.142 --> 00:01:43.402
that led you to not playing basketball--

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:45.412
- [Chris] No, no, no,
it was a very dramatic--

00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:46.782
- Why don't you give
them a little context?

00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:49.562
- Well I mean, I got blood clots,

00:01:49.562 --> 00:01:53.789
I had a pulmonary embolism
back in 2014, no '15.

00:01:57.662 --> 00:01:58.872
That was really bad.

00:01:58.872 --> 00:02:01.205
I was in the hospital
for damn near two weeks.

00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:04.415
That sucked, had to have surgery--

00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:06.122
- [Gary] Was it dangerous?

00:02:06.122 --> 00:02:08.125
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, blood clots.

00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:11.452
- [Gary] Did you know something was up?

00:02:11.452 --> 00:02:13.069
- It was slowly happening.

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:15.292
I mean I know all the
signs to look for now

00:02:15.292 --> 00:02:18.762
but slowly, I mean, at first
I thought it was cramps

00:02:18.762 --> 00:02:20.722
and then a month later they come back

00:02:20.722 --> 00:02:22.572
and then it became so debilitating,

00:02:22.572 --> 00:02:25.902
I'm like, yo, I got to go
to, there is something wrong.

00:02:25.902 --> 00:02:27.875
But luckily made it through that

00:02:27.875 --> 00:02:31.025
and then bounced back,
got back into playing,

00:02:32.162 --> 00:02:35.252
got back to my form of playing

00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:36.812
the way where I was happy and everything

00:02:36.812 --> 00:02:37.912
and then after that

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.912
my calf was sore I go to
the doctor to get a scan,

00:02:42.912 --> 00:02:43.745
oh I got blood clots again

00:02:43.745 --> 00:02:46.405
and then it became a
problem because I felt good,

00:02:47.422 --> 00:02:49.902
I just everything just was just stopped

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:52.586
all of a sudden one day and

00:02:52.586 --> 00:02:55.750
yeah, I wasn't a happy camper.
- And you're 31 years old

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.759
at the time, 31, 32.
- At the time I was 33,

00:02:57.759 --> 00:03:00.602
32, 33, somewhere in there yeah.

00:03:00.602 --> 00:03:03.502
- [Gary] Still feeling like
you had three, four, five, six?

00:03:03.502 --> 00:03:05.102
- Oh man, six, seven left in me.

00:03:05.102 --> 00:03:06.852
I would still be playing right now.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.482
I wanted to play till I was 36

00:03:10.482 --> 00:03:12.682
but I could have potentially

00:03:12.682 --> 00:03:14.602
with the way things are
going in the league now--

00:03:14.602 --> 00:03:17.292
- [Gary] When's the first
time somebody said to you,

00:03:17.292 --> 00:03:19.922
hey you may want to consider not playing?

00:03:19.922 --> 00:03:21.552
- Nobody ever said it.

00:03:21.552 --> 00:03:23.089
A lot of people said it but--

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:25.122
- Now you're starting to sound like me,

00:03:25.122 --> 00:03:27.307
nobody said it, everybody said it,

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:28.322
that's the shit I do.

00:03:28.322 --> 00:03:29.765
- [Chris] I didn't listen to it.

00:03:30.632 --> 00:03:33.452
- SPO, is this true, do I have this right,

00:03:33.452 --> 00:03:36.482
SPO was at the game when
Hank Gathers passed?

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.315
- [Chris] Yeah.

00:03:37.315 --> 00:03:38.622
- I think that's a fact

00:03:38.622 --> 00:03:39.455
that a lot of people don't know.

00:03:39.455 --> 00:03:42.042
Your coach at the time, I'm 43,

00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:46.132
I was outrageously affected
by Hank Gathers passing.

00:03:46.132 --> 00:03:47.162
I was in eighth grade,

00:03:47.162 --> 00:03:49.432
super into the March Madness thing,

00:03:49.432 --> 00:03:52.452
Bo Kimble, like it was a big deal,

00:03:52.452 --> 00:03:53.562
no internet back then,

00:03:53.562 --> 00:03:57.022
big events really took
over America for a week.

00:03:57.022 --> 00:04:00.522
Now Prince dies and it's
a half a day on Twitter,

00:04:00.522 --> 00:04:01.792
like real big stuff happens

00:04:01.792 --> 00:04:02.672
and it's a half a day

00:04:02.672 --> 00:04:04.342
with the way our world works now.

00:04:04.342 --> 00:04:07.472
Back then, something big
happens, it was a week,

00:04:07.472 --> 00:04:08.915
two weeks in the news.

00:04:11.482 --> 00:04:13.582
Was coach affected by that
when he communicated with you?

00:04:13.582 --> 00:04:14.622
I always wondered about that.

00:04:14.622 --> 00:04:15.455
I know coach a little.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:16.942
He's a great dude.

00:04:18.662 --> 00:04:21.322
- I think that was kind of
everybody else's thought process

00:04:21.322 --> 00:04:22.272
going through that and saying

00:04:22.272 --> 00:04:24.792
oh wow this is dire situation.

00:04:24.792 --> 00:04:29.792
With me, you don't necessarily
tell that person that right?

00:04:30.052 --> 00:04:32.622
You want them to kind of exercise things,

00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:33.702
yeah get there by themselves.

00:04:33.702 --> 00:04:34.912
We never talked

00:04:34.912 --> 00:04:37.562
about it too much.
- What about doctors?

00:04:37.562 --> 00:04:38.752
Were like don't play?

00:04:38.752 --> 00:04:41.462
- Yeah, they were, oh, you can't play,

00:04:41.462 --> 00:04:42.722
you'll be fine sir,

00:04:42.722 --> 00:04:44.882
like you've had your life, bro.

00:04:44.882 --> 00:04:47.412
I'm just really getting
started with this stuff

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:49.082
and you're telling me that,

00:04:49.082 --> 00:04:51.162
that's kind of where the
disagreements came in

00:04:51.162 --> 00:04:53.462
with the communication and
our problem with communication

00:04:53.462 --> 00:04:54.872
but we got over that

00:04:54.872 --> 00:04:59.632
and it was just my stance was
to continue to try and play

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.224
And that's what I did for
about a year and a half.

00:05:02.224 --> 00:05:03.244
- [Gary] And then?

00:05:03.244 --> 00:05:06.714
- Then I was like, yeah,
I'm not gonna do this.

00:05:06.714 --> 00:05:07.547
- How did that go down?

00:05:07.547 --> 00:05:09.662
Did you just wake up one morning?

00:05:09.662 --> 00:05:11.372
- I tell people this
story, I was watching,

00:05:11.372 --> 00:05:13.412
so I was in the next year,

00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:14.552
a whole year went by I said,

00:05:14.552 --> 00:05:16.192
all right I know I'm gonna
get picked up this year,

00:05:16.192 --> 00:05:17.472
I need to get into basketball,

00:05:17.472 --> 00:05:18.782
I need to watch more basketball

00:05:18.782 --> 00:05:21.102
'cause I kind of let it go last year.

00:05:21.102 --> 00:05:23.332
I was still working out
but didn't watch any games.

00:05:23.332 --> 00:05:25.252
- [Gary] 'Cause you
just needed that break?

00:05:25.252 --> 00:05:27.092
- Just mentally it was messing with me.

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:27.925
It hurts 'cause--

00:05:27.925 --> 00:05:28.758
- [Gary] You wanted to be out there?

00:05:28.758 --> 00:05:29.652
- Yeah, I was right there

00:05:29.652 --> 00:05:31.262
and I felt that we had a
championship-caliber team

00:05:31.262 --> 00:05:33.802
and then it just stops one day.

00:05:33.802 --> 00:05:37.622
Then I saw Gordon Hayward
dislocate his ankle

00:05:37.622 --> 00:05:38.455
in the first five minutes.

00:05:38.455 --> 00:05:39.352
- [Gary] I was at that game.

00:05:39.352 --> 00:05:40.389
- You was at that game?

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:41.382
- [Gary] I was at that game.

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:42.222
Luckily, on the other side.

00:05:42.222 --> 00:05:45.032
- I just turned the TV on
like all right here we go

00:05:45.032 --> 00:05:47.955
the season's going on, bloop bloop.

00:05:48.852 --> 00:05:50.092
- [Gary] You almost threw up.

00:05:50.092 --> 00:05:52.142
- Oh man I said I right
right then and there

00:05:52.142 --> 00:05:53.722
I knew I wasn't gonna play anymore.

00:05:53.722 --> 00:05:54.555
- [Gary] Really?

00:05:54.555 --> 00:05:55.388
- Yeah, I knew.

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:58.222
And I had to keep up with the Jones.

00:05:58.222 --> 00:05:59.899
I told everybody I was gonna keep playing,

00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:00.732
"You coming back?"

00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:01.843
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:06:01.843 --> 00:06:02.676
- But you knew?

00:06:02.676 --> 00:06:03.509
- [Chris] But I knew right then

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:04.759
it wasn't gonna happen no more.

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:05.672
- Really?

00:06:05.672 --> 00:06:07.352
- [Chris] Yeah, it's just like,

00:06:07.352 --> 00:06:08.272
you know that thing,

00:06:08.272 --> 00:06:09.932
I was there, we were strong

00:06:09.932 --> 00:06:13.122
and then boy it took all
the wind out my cell.

00:06:13.122 --> 00:06:13.955
- No kidding.

00:06:13.955 --> 00:06:14.909
- [Chris] Yeah, I was done after that.

00:06:14.909 --> 00:06:18.372
- And so then take me
through the next chapter.

00:06:18.372 --> 00:06:21.072
So now yourself, you know you're done,

00:06:21.072 --> 00:06:22.412
you keep up the facade a little bit

00:06:22.412 --> 00:06:23.992
because it's a hard left

00:06:23.992 --> 00:06:26.652
and so you want to go
through that in what way yeah

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:28.865
and then what do you
start thinking about next?

00:06:28.865 --> 00:06:31.504
- Shh man what am I doing, you know?

00:06:31.504 --> 00:06:32.761
That's not an easy question.

00:06:32.761 --> 00:06:33.972
- [Gary] Do you go into
like a depressed state?

00:06:33.972 --> 00:06:37.012
- I think I was already
there at that point already.

00:06:37.012 --> 00:06:38.272
It took about two years,

00:06:38.272 --> 00:06:40.352
I want to say two years
after I was done playing,

00:06:40.352 --> 00:06:41.882
it took me that long to kind of

00:06:41.882 --> 00:06:43.532
come out the fog a little bit.

00:06:43.532 --> 00:06:44.365
- [Gary] I understand.

00:06:44.365 --> 00:06:46.812
- Yeah and I guess yeah
that's what people call

00:06:46.812 --> 00:06:48.157
a depression or something.

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:48.990
- You know it's funny,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.302
my brother AJ, who's sitting
right there, left Vayner,

00:06:51.302 --> 00:06:53.082
we started this together, VaynerMedia

00:06:53.082 --> 00:06:55.312
and then three years ago,
three and a half years ago,

00:06:55.312 --> 00:06:58.022
we started a sports representation
business, VaynerSports.

00:06:58.022 --> 00:06:58.949
We do football now

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:01.892
and we have 20 guys, 25 guys

00:07:01.892 --> 00:07:06.852
and our entire thesis was off-the-field

00:07:06.852 --> 00:07:08.462
dynamics that we could create

00:07:08.462 --> 00:07:10.972
but a lot of it was life after football.

00:07:10.972 --> 00:07:13.417
These kids are, first
of all football's crazy,

00:07:13.417 --> 00:07:15.682
because a lot of these guys
are top of their class--

00:07:15.682 --> 00:07:16.515
- [Chris] Way crazier.

00:07:16.515 --> 00:07:17.972
- Yeah I mean there's
real injuries in football.

00:07:17.972 --> 00:07:19.342
There's real stuff going on.

00:07:19.342 --> 00:07:21.582
Kids are, the first round picks

00:07:21.582 --> 00:07:23.572
out of the league three years
later for real for real.

00:07:23.572 --> 00:07:26.905
So that's 25, that's 26.

00:07:27.932 --> 00:07:30.122
- [Chris] What do they do usually?

00:07:30.122 --> 00:07:31.722
What's the next step in that?

00:07:31.722 --> 00:07:34.612
- Most kids really screw up.

00:07:34.612 --> 00:07:36.922
They you overspend their money up front,

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:41.712
then they're done, they're
25 and they're like,

00:07:41.712 --> 00:07:43.462
I mean this is why we did it,

00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:45.562
we're like we think we can change it.

00:07:45.562 --> 00:07:49.062
I watched, he watched
from afar that agents,

00:07:49.062 --> 00:07:50.692
and I don't even blame agents,

00:07:50.692 --> 00:07:51.622
it's not like I razz them,

00:07:51.622 --> 00:07:53.022
I don't think these are bad people,

00:07:53.022 --> 00:07:54.122
they're in business.

00:07:54.122 --> 00:07:57.682
Agents are there and then when
you're not producing dollars

00:07:57.682 --> 00:08:00.062
it's their responsibility to
their company and their family

00:08:00.062 --> 00:08:02.052
to go find things that
do and they're done.

00:08:02.052 --> 00:08:04.062
And we thought the way to hack it

00:08:04.062 --> 00:08:07.912
was really be in these kids
lives through and through.

00:08:07.912 --> 00:08:10.592
And obviously our business
acumen and all that other stuff

00:08:10.592 --> 00:08:13.532
gave us a leverage that they
would potentially believe us

00:08:13.532 --> 00:08:15.142
and now that we're starting
to do it a little bit,

00:08:15.142 --> 00:08:16.302
Walter Pal, Derek Morgan,

00:08:16.302 --> 00:08:18.992
we're doing real stuff for these gentlemen

00:08:18.992 --> 00:08:21.022
long after football when
they brought us no dollars

00:08:21.022 --> 00:08:22.702
'cause we came in late in their contracts,

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:24.202
now the reputation's working

00:08:24.202 --> 00:08:25.642
which is giving us leverage

00:08:25.642 --> 00:08:28.072
but going back to you, that's the thing,

00:08:28.072 --> 00:08:30.042
like as a kid,

00:08:30.042 --> 00:08:32.122
even right now as a 43-year-old male

00:08:32.122 --> 00:08:34.162
who puts out content every day

00:08:34.162 --> 00:08:37.222
that yells at people that says
you're 54 and you're young,

00:08:37.222 --> 00:08:40.312
I still think athletes
that retire are old.

00:08:40.312 --> 00:08:42.862
Like when an athlete retires,
I'm like oh, they're old

00:08:42.862 --> 00:08:44.342
'cause that's the way we grew up.

00:08:44.342 --> 00:08:46.792
- So garden, we're looking
at the garden right there.

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.422
Last week the Hawks
played a preseason game,

00:08:49.422 --> 00:08:50.255
Vince is 43,

00:08:51.809 --> 00:08:53.912
and I've been telling
people this whole story,

00:08:53.912 --> 00:08:55.562
it's like a running joke.
- Like you grew up

00:08:55.562 --> 00:08:56.395
loving Vince as a kid.

00:08:56.395 --> 00:08:57.692
He's still out there.

00:08:57.692 --> 00:09:01.535
- Vince Carter's so old, how old is he?

00:09:02.452 --> 00:09:05.435
Like everywhere he goes and it's just,

00:09:05.435 --> 00:09:06.292
and that's on TV, man.

00:09:06.292 --> 00:09:07.872
You know how powerful TV is.

00:09:07.872 --> 00:09:11.362
- And he's not, and he's
not, and to your point.

00:09:11.362 --> 00:09:13.352
Now what's great,

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:14.672
I think the best thing that's happened

00:09:14.672 --> 00:09:16.972
in the culture of sports

00:09:16.972 --> 00:09:19.222
is that athletes have become,

00:09:19.222 --> 00:09:22.502
when I was growing up
athletes only wanted to

00:09:22.502 --> 00:09:26.419
maybe also or be rappers
if they weren't athletes.

00:09:26.419 --> 00:09:28.542
Mow they want to be entrepreneurs too.

00:09:28.542 --> 00:09:29.572
And that is completely,

00:09:29.572 --> 00:09:33.242
I mean I've got five star
recruits when they're 16

00:09:33.242 --> 00:09:34.162
hitting me up talking

00:09:34.162 --> 00:09:36.432
about life after basketball and football.

00:09:36.432 --> 00:09:39.822
So it is now cool, it is now legit

00:09:39.822 --> 00:09:42.865
and it also feeds a little bit
of their competitive spirit.

00:09:43.994 --> 00:09:45.832
There's a lot of things that make me worry

00:09:45.832 --> 00:09:47.172
that entrepreneurship has gotten cool

00:09:47.172 --> 00:09:48.912
because I think a lot of
people are getting into it

00:09:48.912 --> 00:09:50.312
that aren't entrepreneurs

00:09:50.312 --> 00:09:52.142
and unlike basketball and rap,

00:09:52.142 --> 00:09:53.602
they can't be exposed real quick

00:09:53.602 --> 00:09:54.435
and they get into like

00:09:54.435 --> 00:09:57.252
their own little deep depression over time

00:09:57.252 --> 00:09:58.932
because it's not meant to be.

00:09:58.932 --> 00:10:01.642
But one thing I love about
the rise entrepreneurship

00:10:01.642 --> 00:10:03.832
is it absolutely I believe

00:10:03.832 --> 00:10:06.592
something that is going
to help a ton of athletes

00:10:06.592 --> 00:10:08.182
in that transition.

00:10:08.182 --> 00:10:09.192
- [Chris] No absolutely.

00:10:09.192 --> 00:10:12.062
- So talk to me about back
to the first question,

00:10:12.062 --> 00:10:12.895
what are you up to now?

00:10:12.895 --> 00:10:15.724
You get out of the fog and now?

00:10:15.724 --> 00:10:19.542
- Now, just trying to
figure things out really.

00:10:19.542 --> 00:10:20.747
- [Gary] What's interesting to you?

00:10:20.747 --> 00:10:21.580
- I've been in Japan,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:22.512
I've been to Australia.

00:10:22.512 --> 00:10:23.775
Media interests me.

00:10:25.052 --> 00:10:29.092
I'm working on a book for
a 2020 release in the fall

00:10:29.092 --> 00:10:29.925
for next year.

00:10:29.925 --> 00:10:31.312
That's going great.

00:10:31.312 --> 00:10:32.823
I just started getting out there--

00:10:32.823 --> 00:10:33.656
- [Gary] Are you working
with a ghost writer?

00:10:33.656 --> 00:10:34.532
- Yeah.

00:10:34.532 --> 00:10:36.122
- My ghost writer
actually lives in Austin.

00:10:36.122 --> 00:10:37.783
Yeah, she's amazing.

00:10:37.783 --> 00:10:41.202
And so how's that process been, good?

00:10:41.202 --> 00:10:43.225
Are you recording, are you interviewing?

00:10:43.225 --> 00:10:44.909
- Yeah, recording, a
little bit of everything,

00:10:44.909 --> 00:10:47.352
recording, typing, talking, interviewing.

00:10:47.352 --> 00:10:48.402
It's like a whole process

00:10:48.402 --> 00:10:50.945
but it's like a chemistry that you build.

00:10:51.892 --> 00:10:53.422
- Let me just bounce
around a little bit here.

00:10:53.422 --> 00:10:56.689
When did you think you
were gonna make the league?

00:10:57.659 --> 00:11:00.295
- I knew I was gonna make
the league when I was 17.

00:11:01.172 --> 00:11:04.112
The summer going into my senior
year, yeah, I knew, yeah.

00:11:04.112 --> 00:11:05.952
- Prior to that there
was still kind of like,

00:11:05.952 --> 00:11:07.168
'cause it's such a big,

00:11:07.168 --> 00:11:08.432
actually that's great,

00:11:08.432 --> 00:11:10.422
let's keep that on the shelf for a second,

00:11:10.422 --> 00:11:12.182
when did you want to go into the league?

00:11:12.182 --> 00:11:15.562
- Probably when I was like six
or seven, pretty early, yeah,

00:11:15.562 --> 00:11:16.562
very, very early

00:11:16.562 --> 00:11:17.805
'cause my dad was watching--

00:11:17.805 --> 00:11:18.872
- Were you a Mavericks fan?

00:11:18.872 --> 00:11:20.252
- No, the Mavericks were really bad.

00:11:20.252 --> 00:11:21.652
- I'm aware.

00:11:21.652 --> 00:11:22.485
- Yeah, they were really, really bad.

00:11:22.485 --> 00:11:24.422
- You're like every
other NBA fan bandwagon?

00:11:24.422 --> 00:11:27.262
- Yeah, yeah, 'cause
MJ was on TV every day.

00:11:27.262 --> 00:11:30.276
So in the, at first I
remember like thinking

00:11:30.276 --> 00:11:32.282
like who is this guy in this same team

00:11:32.282 --> 00:11:34.292
'cause every time I go over my
grandma's house in the summer

00:11:34.292 --> 00:11:35.612
they're always playing.

00:11:35.612 --> 00:11:37.992
And then everybody would be
around the TV watching it

00:11:37.992 --> 00:11:39.712
and my dad would be doing the same thing.

00:11:39.712 --> 00:11:41.582
So I said, yo I want do that.

00:11:41.582 --> 00:11:44.012
This is the same game that
we play on the playground

00:11:44.012 --> 00:11:46.242
and oh it's on TV and
then after that I said,

00:11:46.242 --> 00:11:47.669
all right cool that's what I want to do.

00:11:47.669 --> 00:11:50.033
And it was always there but then--

00:11:50.033 --> 00:11:51.742
- Was there anybody in your
family that was really tall

00:11:51.742 --> 00:11:53.592
or athletic or things of that nature?

00:11:54.792 --> 00:11:55.682
- Me.

00:11:55.682 --> 00:11:56.912
- [Gary] Really, you're an enigma?

00:11:56.912 --> 00:11:59.852
My dad is like 6'3, I mean I
have some couple tall uncles

00:11:59.852 --> 00:12:02.312
but nothing, I don't know
where I came from, yeah.

00:12:02.312 --> 00:12:04.322
I'm just a little, little taller.

00:12:04.322 --> 00:12:06.592
- [Gary] And when did
you explode in height?

00:12:06.592 --> 00:12:08.462
- From 14 to 16, thanks man.

00:12:08.462 --> 00:12:10.812
- [Gary] You went from what to what?

00:12:10.812 --> 00:12:14.352
- I went from like 6'3
to 6'10, 6'2 to 6'10,

00:12:14.352 --> 00:12:15.382
somewhere in there.

00:12:15.382 --> 00:12:18.432
So my freshman year when
I got there as a freshman,

00:12:18.432 --> 00:12:20.162
I was about 6'1, 6'2.

00:12:20.162 --> 00:12:22.975
I left about 6'11 prospect.

00:12:24.232 --> 00:12:28.095
So 14 to 16 is like oh I
really want to be in the NBA

00:12:28.095 --> 00:12:30.282
and I was just practicing really hard,

00:12:30.282 --> 00:12:31.132
doing what I needed to do

00:12:31.132 --> 00:12:34.592
and then 17, 18, the height came with it,

00:12:34.592 --> 00:12:36.282
19, I was out of there.

00:12:36.282 --> 00:12:37.874
So it happened fairly quickly.

00:12:37.874 --> 00:12:38.902
- Yeah, it did.
- Yeah.

00:12:38.902 --> 00:12:41.222
It was like oh man I'mma play in the NBA,

00:12:41.222 --> 00:12:42.852
I'mma do all these cool things

00:12:42.852 --> 00:12:45.002
and then they said,
aight, it's time to go.

00:12:46.771 --> 00:12:48.242
I wasn't sure I was ready but

00:12:48.242 --> 00:12:50.122
I had to do it.
- What a crazy draft class

00:12:50.122 --> 00:12:50.955
you were part of.

00:12:50.955 --> 00:12:51.912
- [Chris] Yeah it was crazy.

00:12:51.912 --> 00:12:53.182
- Like super crazy.

00:12:53.182 --> 00:12:54.282
It's actually the one time,

00:12:54.282 --> 00:12:56.298
I've been telling a lot of people lately

00:12:56.298 --> 00:12:59.322
that flip sneakers and
entrepreneurs and hustlers

00:12:59.322 --> 00:13:00.452
to get into sports cards

00:13:00.452 --> 00:13:02.512
'cause it's getting
really, really, really hot

00:13:02.512 --> 00:13:03.782
and I did that as a kid

00:13:03.782 --> 00:13:08.309
and the one time I got back
into it was your draft class

00:13:08.309 --> 00:13:09.995
because there was so many
of you guys, I was like--

00:13:09.995 --> 00:13:11.902
- [Chris] You got some cards?

00:13:11.902 --> 00:13:13.962
- I mean AJ and I were buying so,

00:13:13.962 --> 00:13:17.332
we were opening up so much
FLIR basketball your year,

00:13:17.332 --> 00:13:18.792
there was so much

00:13:18.792 --> 00:13:21.252
'cause we were like that was just loaded.

00:13:21.252 --> 00:13:22.932
I mean it's an all-time class.

00:13:22.932 --> 00:13:26.042
- [Chris] Yeah for sure, it's pretty good.

00:13:26.042 --> 00:13:28.652
- How did your friendship
graph work with that class?

00:13:28.652 --> 00:13:30.952
Like you guys were obviously
traveling around together,

00:13:30.952 --> 00:13:33.022
well for a couple weeks
there for the draft stuff

00:13:33.022 --> 00:13:35.542
but when did you first make
your connections with your,

00:13:35.542 --> 00:13:37.592
who were your best friends in the league?

00:13:40.943 --> 00:13:43.305
- What just period overall?

00:13:43.305 --> 00:13:44.922
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:44.922 --> 00:13:46.325
- Pretty much everybody,

00:13:47.166 --> 00:13:49.691
the whole championship
team was an amazing team.

00:13:49.691 --> 00:13:51.002
Everybody got along,

00:13:51.002 --> 00:13:54.022
everybody made great connections.

00:13:54.022 --> 00:13:57.519
Ronny Turiaf's a great
friend of mine, Jerry Jack--

00:13:57.519 --> 00:13:58.352
- You want to hear something funny?

00:13:58.352 --> 00:14:00.102
I have a Ronny Turiaf Jets jersey.

00:14:00.102 --> 00:14:02.122
- How do you have a
Ronny Turiaf Jets jersey?

00:14:02.122 --> 00:14:03.832
- Because when he came to the Knicks

00:14:03.832 --> 00:14:04.932
and he actually made us,

00:14:04.932 --> 00:14:06.282
like when he came to the Knicks,

00:14:06.282 --> 00:14:08.727
our entire friend group got pumped

00:14:08.727 --> 00:14:09.810
because he was bringing back

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.636
a little bit of that '90s Knicks.

00:14:11.636 --> 00:14:12.932
He was being like a tough guy

00:14:12.932 --> 00:14:16.162
and so for birthdays
we're always challenged

00:14:17.111 --> 00:14:19.159
to give each other gifts
that are like unique.

00:14:19.159 --> 00:14:20.652
So did you give me that, AJ?

00:14:20.652 --> 00:14:24.402
So AJ got me a Ronny Turiaf
Jets jersey, number 14 was it?

00:14:24.402 --> 00:14:28.707
Yeah, so that's how we go about that man,

00:14:28.707 --> 00:14:30.716
plus, he knows about
wine, so I fuck with him.

00:14:30.716 --> 00:14:32.902
- Oh yeah that's my
guy, yeah that's my man.

00:14:32.902 --> 00:14:33.952
Jerry Jack's my guy.

00:14:33.952 --> 00:14:35.612
He was my roommate at Georgia Tech

00:14:35.612 --> 00:14:37.829
and we played together for a year.

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:38.683
- He had a real nice career.

00:14:38.683 --> 00:14:39.516
- Yeah, he had a great career

00:14:39.516 --> 00:14:41.757
and I think he's, I'm not sure
if he's still trying to play.

00:14:41.757 --> 00:14:42.920
I haven't talked to him in a minute.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:44.992
- He had a really nice
career, he really did.

00:14:44.992 --> 00:14:45.825
- Yeah.

00:14:45.825 --> 00:14:47.762
- Who was your best mentor
when you came to Toronto?

00:14:47.762 --> 00:14:50.312
Did anybody kind of like
take you under your wing?

00:14:50.312 --> 00:14:51.145
- Yeah it was a few people.

00:14:51.145 --> 00:14:52.482
One was Michael Curry.

00:14:52.482 --> 00:14:55.312
He was my vet coming in.

00:14:55.312 --> 00:14:57.895
He was like a no-nonsense dad type, man.

00:14:59.376 --> 00:15:02.315
He taught me the value of working hard

00:15:02.315 --> 00:15:03.882
and getting your shots up.

00:15:03.882 --> 00:15:08.882
Derrick Martin was a
guy, he did a lot for me.

00:15:10.265 --> 00:15:11.098
Sam Mitchell.
- You had some vets

00:15:11.098 --> 00:15:11.931
on that team.

00:15:11.931 --> 00:15:12.764
- Yeah we had some vets.

00:15:12.764 --> 00:15:13.597
That wasn't all at the same time.

00:15:13.597 --> 00:15:17.572
Michael Curry was only
there my rookie year.

00:15:17.572 --> 00:15:20.335
Mike, Derek and Sam,

00:15:21.452 --> 00:15:23.626
they pretty much took me under their wing

00:15:23.626 --> 00:15:24.517
as soon as they got there.

00:15:24.517 --> 00:15:27.525
I want to say the next year,
second through the sixth year.

00:15:27.525 --> 00:15:29.245
- So you grew up a Bulls fan.

00:15:29.245 --> 00:15:30.092
- [Chris] No, I wasn't a Bulls fan.

00:15:30.092 --> 00:15:31.522
- Just an MJ fan?

00:15:31.522 --> 00:15:33.085
- I was a basketball fan,

00:15:33.923 --> 00:15:35.532
just the Mavericks were just,

00:15:35.532 --> 00:15:36.709
it was 16 and whatever that year

00:15:36.709 --> 00:15:39.032
so it was tough kind of having

00:15:39.032 --> 00:15:41.107
some support for the home team.

00:15:41.107 --> 00:15:42.182
- Yes, I'm aware.

00:15:42.182 --> 00:15:43.015
And what about now?

00:15:43.015 --> 00:15:46.546
Do you literally root for
the Heat and the Raptors?

00:15:46.546 --> 00:15:47.379
Like were you pumped for the Raptors?

00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:49.900
- Yeah, I was happy for those guys, man.

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:50.832
There was still so many people,

00:15:50.832 --> 00:15:55.352
the managers and equipment
guys, popcorn guys

00:15:55.352 --> 00:15:56.932
and the dudes in the stands

00:15:56.932 --> 00:15:58.662
that I would talk to every morning,

00:15:58.662 --> 00:16:01.822
they were they were
still there and I mean,

00:16:01.822 --> 00:16:04.009
it's funny every time I walked in,

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:05.671
they were like, Chris can you believe it?

00:16:05.671 --> 00:16:06.504
I'm like, no I can't can't,

00:16:06.504 --> 00:16:08.772
I cannot at all, this is crazy.

00:16:08.772 --> 00:16:12.265
And then to see 'em really
get their moment was amazing.

00:16:13.465 --> 00:16:15.141
But I root for those guys.

00:16:15.141 --> 00:16:15.974
I root for Miami.

00:16:15.974 --> 00:16:17.892
I identify with the teams I've played for.

00:16:18.829 --> 00:16:19.687
- Of course.

00:16:19.687 --> 00:16:20.520
What about other sports?

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:21.904
Did you grow up a Cowboys fan?

00:16:21.904 --> 00:16:22.737
- Yeah, I'm a Cowboys fan, yeah.

00:16:22.737 --> 00:16:25.081
- That's my only win
this year for the Jets.

00:16:25.081 --> 00:16:25.914
- Yeah, it's cool.

00:16:25.914 --> 00:16:27.339
Everybody's always like, ah,

00:16:28.722 --> 00:16:31.172
you always get that all the time.

00:16:31.172 --> 00:16:32.232
Look, I'm from Dallas,

00:16:32.232 --> 00:16:34.472
I grew up during the glory years

00:16:34.472 --> 00:16:37.335
and it's a good franchise.

00:16:38.312 --> 00:16:41.107
- Did all your homies
from back home razz you

00:16:41.107 --> 00:16:41.972
because of that NBA Finals?

00:16:41.972 --> 00:16:42.805
That must've been

00:16:42.805 --> 00:16:43.638
fucking terrible.
- Which part?

00:16:43.638 --> 00:16:45.272
- Losing to the Mavericks
in the NBA finals.

00:16:45.272 --> 00:16:46.422
- Oh, no, they didn't.

00:16:46.422 --> 00:16:48.612
If they did, we haven't spoken since.

00:16:48.612 --> 00:16:49.445
- [Gary] Really?

00:16:49.445 --> 00:16:50.512
- Yeah, no, we didn't take it,

00:16:50.512 --> 00:16:52.165
we take it very seriously.
- I was thinking to myself,

00:16:52.165 --> 00:16:53.262
man he loses in Dallas,

00:16:53.262 --> 00:16:55.132
he loses, obviously that was very--

00:16:55.132 --> 00:16:56.102
- [Chris] That really sucked, by the way.

00:16:56.102 --> 00:16:57.392
- Of course, that was super disappointing

00:16:57.392 --> 00:16:59.312
but you got those three or four friends

00:16:59.312 --> 00:17:00.332
that are your boys boys

00:17:00.332 --> 00:17:02.892
who might've actually grew
up cheering for the Mavericks

00:17:02.892 --> 00:17:03.725
since they were--

00:17:03.725 --> 00:17:04.558
- [Chris] No.

00:17:04.558 --> 00:17:05.439
- No.

00:17:05.439 --> 00:17:06.522
- None of us.

00:17:07.968 --> 00:17:09.201
We were all hurtin'.

00:17:09.201 --> 00:17:10.172
I mean I wouldn't even call it bandwagon.

00:17:10.172 --> 00:17:11.005
- It's bandwagon.

00:17:11.005 --> 00:17:12.152
- [Chris] I don't have a team.

00:17:12.152 --> 00:17:13.422
- Fair enough but like--

00:17:13.422 --> 00:17:14.629
- [Chris] We don't have a wagon.

00:17:14.629 --> 00:17:16.822
- It's a variation of a wagon.

00:17:16.822 --> 00:17:18.192
It's an all-star wagon.

00:17:18.192 --> 00:17:19.312
- [Chris] A wagon with no wheels.

00:17:19.312 --> 00:17:20.212
- Here's my thing.

00:17:22.426 --> 00:17:25.392
I got very into tech in the 2000s

00:17:25.392 --> 00:17:28.049
and would go to Silicon Valley
a lot in 2006, '07, '08, '09

00:17:28.049 --> 00:17:29.372
and when I tell you,

00:17:29.372 --> 00:17:30.442
because I'm a big sports fan,

00:17:30.442 --> 00:17:32.922
and this is the time when
it was all nerd land,

00:17:32.922 --> 00:17:34.342
they barely cared about sports

00:17:34.342 --> 00:17:36.952
but when I tell you there was
not a person in San Francisco

00:17:36.952 --> 00:17:39.672
that knew the Warriors played there,

00:17:39.672 --> 00:17:41.872
like there wasn't a person on Earth,

00:17:41.872 --> 00:17:46.752
I had never seen or anybody
wear a jersey of the Warriors

00:17:46.752 --> 00:17:50.652
or mention that word in the
four to five year period

00:17:50.652 --> 00:17:54.132
that I spent 12, 15
times in San Francisco,

00:17:54.132 --> 00:17:58.532
and now every kid in Cleveland
and Ohio and Dallas wears--

00:17:58.532 --> 00:18:01.282
- [Chris] Steph Curry
jersey, shoes, the hat.

00:18:01.282 --> 00:18:02.712
- What do you think about the notion

00:18:02.712 --> 00:18:06.562
of people wrapping their
own lack of self esteem

00:18:06.562 --> 00:18:09.692
into something else like
it's like Patriot fans right,

00:18:09.692 --> 00:18:11.562
like a bunch of them
razzed me on Monday night

00:18:11.562 --> 00:18:12.972
and I was sitting there, I was looking,

00:18:12.972 --> 00:18:15.132
I'm like you did nothing.

00:18:15.132 --> 00:18:17.112
Do not confuse your team's success

00:18:17.112 --> 00:18:19.212
with your actual success.

00:18:19.212 --> 00:18:20.239
Thoughts on that?

00:18:20.239 --> 00:18:22.672
(laughing)

00:18:22.672 --> 00:18:25.102
- I mean there's not much you can say.

00:18:25.102 --> 00:18:27.642
Hey I get it, people want to feel good

00:18:27.642 --> 00:18:32.302
I mean I remember when we
got together with Miami

00:18:32.302 --> 00:18:34.752
and it was more Miami fans,

00:18:34.752 --> 00:18:36.542
we're like, hey that's great.

00:18:36.542 --> 00:18:39.882
Then the same guy'll have
a Cleveland Jersey on

00:18:39.882 --> 00:18:40.752
four years later,

00:18:40.752 --> 00:18:42.292
it's like okay, come on bro,

00:18:42.292 --> 00:18:44.419
you got the hat and the jersey.

00:18:44.419 --> 00:18:45.252
- But that's what you did?

00:18:45.252 --> 00:18:46.085
- [Chris] No, I did not.

00:18:46.085 --> 00:18:46.918
- Yeah you liked players.

00:18:46.918 --> 00:18:48.153
- I liked players, yeah.

00:18:48.153 --> 00:18:49.080
- [Gary] Yeah, well
that's what that guy did.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:49.925
He liked Clay Brian.

00:18:49.925 --> 00:18:50.758
- I could not have their Jersey

00:18:50.758 --> 00:18:53.792
nor could I afford their
stuff or their shoes.

00:18:53.792 --> 00:18:54.962
I had their posters.

00:18:54.962 --> 00:18:56.400
I had the posters on the wall.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:57.550
- The four dollar poster, for sure.

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:58.633
- That was about it.

00:18:58.633 --> 00:18:59.466
- What about video games?

00:18:59.466 --> 00:19:00.299
Were you good at it?

00:19:00.299 --> 00:19:02.182
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, I
play video games for sure.

00:19:02.182 --> 00:19:03.322
- You're strong or?

00:19:03.322 --> 00:19:05.122
- Now, no, no, no, I'm retired.

00:19:05.122 --> 00:19:06.441
- In your prime?

00:19:06.441 --> 00:19:07.274
- In my prime I was pretty good.

00:19:07.274 --> 00:19:08.302
Madden was my game,

00:19:08.302 --> 00:19:12.465
Madden and Call of Duty at one
point in time, Black Ops Two.

00:19:13.422 --> 00:19:14.342
- I love it.

00:19:14.342 --> 00:19:17.632
- I actually bought part
of the Minnesota franchise

00:19:17.632 --> 00:19:19.052
for Call of Duty.

00:19:19.052 --> 00:19:20.632
So that's my little step

00:19:20.632 --> 00:19:22.951
towards ownership of the New York Jets.

00:19:22.951 --> 00:19:24.752
Alright Chris, what else
do you want to talk about?

