00:39:07.025 --> 00:39:07.972
they're a funny fanbase,

00:39:07.972 --> 00:39:09.372
not to razz Miami,

00:39:09.372 --> 00:39:10.972
it's just it's a town that is fun,

00:39:10.972 --> 00:39:13.902
like the ownership for the
Heat is phenomenal to me.

00:39:13.902 --> 00:39:15.635
The fan base is a little funny.

00:39:17.202 --> 00:39:18.792
You guys didn't sell out playoff games.

00:39:18.792 --> 00:39:19.625
That's crazy.

00:39:21.572 --> 00:39:22.452
- It was loud.

00:39:22.452 --> 00:39:24.633
- Yeah, I know it was loud.

00:39:24.633 --> 00:39:26.412
Can we talk about your rebound?

00:39:26.412 --> 00:39:27.245
- [Chris] Yeah sure.

00:39:27.245 --> 00:39:28.078
- Good.

00:39:28.078 --> 00:39:30.132
I believe that your rebound

00:39:30.132 --> 00:39:35.132
is one of the all-time
most underrated plays

00:39:35.232 --> 00:39:37.232
in sports history

00:39:37.232 --> 00:39:40.232
because of the impact it has

00:39:40.232 --> 00:39:43.512
on the enormous passion people have

00:39:43.512 --> 00:39:46.662
debating the goat when
it comes to basketball.

00:39:46.662 --> 00:39:47.892
I mean in its basic form,

00:39:47.892 --> 00:39:50.922
for the non basketball
fans that are listening,

00:39:50.922 --> 00:39:52.982
Chris gets a rebound in an NBA Finals,

00:39:52.982 --> 00:39:54.162
kicks it out to Ray Allen,

00:39:54.162 --> 00:39:57.332
that basically means just
very black-and-white,

00:39:57.332 --> 00:39:59.725
the series ends if he doesn't,

00:40:00.992 --> 00:40:02.312
I was a little passsive with basketball,

00:40:02.312 --> 00:40:04.222
LeBron I think took that shot,

00:40:04.222 --> 00:40:06.472
like that means he would
have missed that shot,

00:40:06.472 --> 00:40:08.122
they would have lost that series,

00:40:09.697 --> 00:40:11.562
that's a big fucking play.

00:40:11.562 --> 00:40:13.382
- Yeah, yeah, kind of.

00:40:13.382 --> 00:40:15.132
Things happen a little differently.

00:40:16.349 --> 00:40:19.222
- Do people, Martin,
thanks so much, brother,

00:40:19.222 --> 00:40:22.119
do people sorry (laughter), no listen--

00:40:23.332 --> 00:40:24.832
- [Chris] Bro, like oh my God.

00:40:27.002 --> 00:40:28.302
- I'm like sports radio,

00:40:28.302 --> 00:40:29.242
I want to get into a point.

00:40:29.242 --> 00:40:30.075
I had to put him off.

00:40:30.075 --> 00:40:32.082
I didn't need him jumping in.

00:40:32.082 --> 00:40:34.522
Chris do a lot of,

00:40:34.522 --> 00:40:36.382
like I'm a hardcore sports fan.

00:40:36.382 --> 00:40:39.342
I've watched every play of a
New York Jets game since 1982.

00:40:39.342 --> 00:40:41.202
So I know every single play.

00:40:41.202 --> 00:40:42.085
Obviously this is a finals game

00:40:42.085 --> 00:40:43.942
it's a huge play,

00:40:43.942 --> 00:40:47.962
I feel like if I was a Heat fan

00:40:47.962 --> 00:40:50.122
and I came across you at an airport,

00:40:50.122 --> 00:40:52.162
I would be over-the-top compelled,

00:40:52.162 --> 00:40:53.172
I wouldn't want to bother you

00:40:53.172 --> 00:40:54.802
but I would have to find
some way to be like,

00:40:54.802 --> 00:40:57.082
bro, that rebound means everything to me.

00:40:57.082 --> 00:40:59.082
Like do you get a lot of love

00:40:59.082 --> 00:41:01.832
or back to my point,

00:41:01.832 --> 00:41:02.892
that this is one of the great

00:41:02.892 --> 00:41:05.312
underrated plays in NBA history,

00:41:05.312 --> 00:41:07.482
is it just that's the way life is,

00:41:07.482 --> 00:41:10.022
it kind of it's a sneaky
little part of it,

00:41:10.022 --> 00:41:13.092
most people don't go to
that extra layer of thinking

00:41:13.092 --> 00:41:14.986
what actually happened.

00:41:14.986 --> 00:41:18.892
- I think it kind of goes both ways.

00:41:18.892 --> 00:41:21.392
People see it and they'll tell me about it

00:41:21.392 --> 00:41:23.735
and then you have,

00:41:24.792 --> 00:41:27.426
all I wanted to ever
do is make a big play.

00:41:27.426 --> 00:41:28.287
- [Gary] Is that true?

00:41:28.287 --> 00:41:29.120
- Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:31.512
I mean as a kid growing up--

00:41:31.512 --> 00:41:33.626
- You probably did game seven, NBA finals,

00:41:33.626 --> 00:41:34.459
two seconds--

00:41:34.459 --> 00:41:35.982
- Oh my goodness, one,
two, one, you missed it,

00:41:35.982 --> 00:41:37.320
then say, oh, it's overtime.

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:38.659
- No, he got fouled.

00:41:38.659 --> 00:41:41.022
- You play those games
and stuff like that,

00:41:41.022 --> 00:41:42.562
the ironic part for me

00:41:42.562 --> 00:41:45.552
was that it was really an exercise for me

00:41:45.552 --> 00:41:47.432
because I didn't score in that game.

00:41:47.432 --> 00:41:49.182
No, game six I did.

00:41:49.182 --> 00:41:52.052
It was just a struggle for me offensively

00:41:52.052 --> 00:41:53.642
in that whole series.

00:41:53.642 --> 00:41:54.942
- [Gary] You were just off with yourself

00:41:54.942 --> 00:41:55.852
or they defended you well?

00:41:55.852 --> 00:41:56.685
- Yeah, they defended well,

00:41:56.685 --> 00:41:58.576
I mean it's the best team in the world

00:41:58.576 --> 00:42:01.342
and the way they were defending us,

00:42:01.342 --> 00:42:05.232
they pretty much, LeBron
and Dwayne had to carry us.

00:42:05.232 --> 00:42:06.522
They stuck to me,

00:42:06.522 --> 00:42:07.782
didn't really rotate much

00:42:07.782 --> 00:42:09.182
and it was working.

00:42:09.182 --> 00:42:10.792
- Who was sticking to you, Tim?

00:42:10.792 --> 00:42:12.122
- Yeah, me and Tim was pretty much

00:42:12.122 --> 00:42:13.332
matched up the whole series

00:42:13.332 --> 00:42:14.212
but the way

00:42:14.212 --> 00:42:15.412
they were doing it--
- Is he a great human being?

00:42:15.412 --> 00:42:16.663
He seems like a nice guy.

00:42:16.663 --> 00:42:17.496
You don't know him?

00:42:17.496 --> 00:42:18.329
- I'm sure he is.

00:42:18.329 --> 00:42:19.537
- [Gary] He's quiet as fuck, right?

00:42:19.537 --> 00:42:21.162
- Yeah, yeah, he's quiet,
phenomenal basketball player.

00:42:21.162 --> 00:42:22.302
- [Gary] Clearly.

00:42:22.302 --> 00:42:23.272
- And great coach I'm sure

00:42:23.272 --> 00:42:24.872
but at the time it was just kind of,

00:42:24.872 --> 00:42:26.682
it was a very tough series

00:42:26.682 --> 00:42:30.542
and everything happened so fast.

00:42:30.542 --> 00:42:32.871
We were winning and we were
in control of that game

00:42:32.871 --> 00:42:34.862
with two minutes left.

00:42:34.862 --> 00:42:36.122
We get into a situation

00:42:36.122 --> 00:42:38.322
where we've got Bron on Tony Parker.

00:42:38.322 --> 00:42:41.142
That was our game plan for
the late game situations.

00:42:41.142 --> 00:42:43.352
All right, we've executed it perfectly,

00:42:43.352 --> 00:42:45.322
Tony hits that step-back three,

00:42:45.322 --> 00:42:46.702
we turn it over a couple times,

00:42:46.702 --> 00:42:48.832
we're down five points.

00:42:48.832 --> 00:42:50.012
It just happens really quick.

00:42:50.012 --> 00:42:52.565
The only thing that kept
me in it was that my wife,

00:42:54.472 --> 00:42:56.732
when you're about to lose a big game,

00:42:56.732 --> 00:42:59.065
you look around at your
family for sympathy.

00:42:59.952 --> 00:43:04.872
It's just like that look
when it's like ah damn

00:43:04.872 --> 00:43:08.732
but I looked at her and
she wasn't looking at me.

00:43:08.732 --> 00:43:10.728
She just stood up and started clapping

00:43:10.728 --> 00:43:13.082
and it's kind of like a shell shock

00:43:13.082 --> 00:43:13.942
when you go to the bench,

00:43:13.942 --> 00:43:16.962
it's kind of like holy shit, oh no,

00:43:16.962 --> 00:43:18.102
and we were just kind of,

00:43:18.102 --> 00:43:20.052
I was looking at everybody else and say

00:43:20.052 --> 00:43:21.845
yeah, this is not a good situation.

00:43:22.962 --> 00:43:24.492
I looked at my wife and she was,

00:43:24.492 --> 00:43:26.332
she just stood up and start clapping

00:43:26.332 --> 00:43:28.312
was like I don't know what
the hell she was looking at

00:43:28.312 --> 00:43:30.452
and so that snapped me back into it

00:43:30.452 --> 00:43:32.002
and we were able to make the plays

00:43:32.002 --> 00:43:34.082
to extend the game and extend the series.

00:43:34.082 --> 00:43:36.335
That rebound just happened 'cause-

00:43:37.442 --> 00:43:39.119
- [Gary] You're playing basketball.

00:43:39.119 --> 00:43:39.952
- Yeah playing basketball.

00:43:39.952 --> 00:43:41.472
- But it's fucking huge.

00:43:41.472 --> 00:43:42.472
- Yeah well I mean,

00:43:42.472 --> 00:43:44.862
I was able to watch the
trajectory of the ball,

00:43:44.862 --> 00:43:45.825
you know what I mean?

00:43:45.825 --> 00:43:47.579
So back some time ago,

00:43:47.579 --> 00:43:49.362
one of my good friends told me,

00:43:49.362 --> 00:43:51.042
hey or a great player told me,

00:43:51.042 --> 00:43:53.482
if you want to get a rebound,

00:43:53.482 --> 00:43:56.292
you get there before the
ball gets there, right?

00:43:56.292 --> 00:43:57.642
So in that situation,

00:43:57.642 --> 00:44:01.902
I could watch, you practice
watching the ball go

00:44:01.902 --> 00:44:04.132
and you just know where
it goes after a while.

00:44:04.132 --> 00:44:07.132
I was able to kind of
watch it the whole way

00:44:07.132 --> 00:44:09.902
and first person I saw was Ray.

00:44:09.902 --> 00:44:10.822
- [Gary] That's a good person to see.

00:44:10.822 --> 00:44:13.062
- Yeah and in my mind it happened very,

00:44:13.062 --> 00:44:14.782
it's like slow-motion, like the Matrix,

00:44:14.782 --> 00:44:15.772
you know what I mean?

00:44:15.772 --> 00:44:17.742
In real time, it still does not--

00:44:17.742 --> 00:44:18.575
- [Gary] Like a hundredth of a second.

00:44:18.575 --> 00:44:20.012
- Yeah it still does not seen
that way in my mind to me.

00:44:20.012 --> 00:44:22.382
Like, I'll watch it, it's like, okay.

00:44:22.382 --> 00:44:26.705
- I genuinely think that that
play is really underrated.

00:44:27.722 --> 00:44:29.662
- I don't even want,

00:44:29.662 --> 00:44:32.832
I like the fact that
it's kind of under there,

00:44:32.832 --> 00:44:36.725
kind of hanging right
there in plain sight,

00:44:37.712 --> 00:44:41.342
because you just hope that you
can make those type of plays

00:44:41.342 --> 00:44:42.485
that people will remember.

00:44:42.485 --> 00:44:45.025
I remember when Jordan
hit all those shots,

00:44:45.025 --> 00:44:48.392
Packson, Steve Kerr, Hakeem Olajuwon.

00:44:48.392 --> 00:44:50.582
- I just think that play
needs to be branded more.

00:44:50.582 --> 00:44:52.882
I really really genuinely think like

00:44:52.882 --> 00:44:55.282
that needs to be more
known as like the rebound.

00:44:55.282 --> 00:44:56.122
It is that big.

00:44:56.122 --> 00:44:57.432
I really do think it's that big

00:44:57.432 --> 00:45:00.812
and honestly and I, listen,
I hate Michael Jordan.

00:45:00.812 --> 00:45:03.202
So I've spent my entire last 15 years

00:45:03.202 --> 00:45:05.852
hoping and wishing and rooting for LeBron

00:45:05.852 --> 00:45:09.352
to do whatever he needs
to do to climb that later.

00:45:09.352 --> 00:45:12.642
It's crazy, my number one
grateful thing towards you

00:45:12.642 --> 00:45:13.812
is that rebound

00:45:13.812 --> 00:45:14.922
because without it

00:45:16.022 --> 00:45:17.172
that game would have been over

00:45:17.172 --> 00:45:18.142
and the series would have been over.

00:45:18.142 --> 00:45:19.282
- Yeah, they probably would've made

00:45:19.282 --> 00:45:20.392
some changes with the team.

00:45:20.392 --> 00:45:21.552
I mean we were fully aware,

00:45:21.552 --> 00:45:23.899
we were fully aware of
what we were up against.

00:45:23.899 --> 00:45:25.682
But I always tell people,

00:45:25.682 --> 00:45:29.332
I'll get mad when somebody
say, well the game's over.

00:45:29.332 --> 00:45:31.132
I get so mad

00:45:31.132 --> 00:45:33.322
because it's like no,
the game is never over

00:45:33.322 --> 00:45:35.730
until you see like triple
zeroes right there.

00:45:35.730 --> 00:45:36.563
You know what I mean?

00:45:36.563 --> 00:45:37.762
Like oh, we'll leave.

00:45:37.762 --> 00:45:39.052
Don't leave.

00:45:39.052 --> 00:45:40.152
- That was really,

00:45:40.152 --> 00:45:42.282
first of all, especially
in my two favorite sports,

00:45:42.282 --> 00:45:43.792
basketball and football
are funny like that,

00:45:43.792 --> 00:45:46.332
like there's just something to,

00:45:46.332 --> 00:45:48.062
actually all of 'em, actually all of 'em.

00:45:48.062 --> 00:45:50.134
That's absolutely right,
that's absolutely right.

00:45:50.134 --> 00:45:50.967
- Yeah, just don't leave man.

00:45:50.967 --> 00:45:52.202
And so any other people

00:45:52.202 --> 00:45:54.732
even you have to stick with things even

00:45:54.732 --> 00:45:57.182
sometimes you'll give up
on yourself or something

00:45:57.182 --> 00:45:59.702
like that when you're right on the cusp--

00:45:59.702 --> 00:46:02.592
- What is the hottest
you were in your career?

00:46:02.592 --> 00:46:04.642
Like when I ask you that question,

00:46:04.642 --> 00:46:07.332
what stretch of time, what period,

00:46:07.332 --> 00:46:08.452
like what two weeks,

00:46:08.452 --> 00:46:10.922
I was always fascinated like fuck,

00:46:10.922 --> 00:46:13.252
it's funny I've been during
the majority of your career

00:46:13.252 --> 00:46:15.472
I was quite passive with
basketball in comparison.

00:46:15.472 --> 00:46:18.332
Knicks went into a coma
and my career took off

00:46:18.332 --> 00:46:21.552
and I was working till
11 p.m. every weekday.

00:46:21.552 --> 00:46:22.385
So unlike football,

00:46:22.385 --> 00:46:24.062
where Sunday I could make the commitment,

00:46:24.062 --> 00:46:25.872
it was hard for me to get all in

00:46:25.872 --> 00:46:28.432
but in my childhood I was super in.

00:46:28.432 --> 00:46:30.012
Actually back to the Spurs,

00:46:30.012 --> 00:46:32.522
I just remember David
Robinson would have pockets

00:46:32.522 --> 00:46:34.302
where he would just go lightning hot

00:46:34.302 --> 00:46:37.362
like it was just really interesting
to watch some people go,

00:46:37.362 --> 00:46:39.082
like when I ask you that question,

00:46:39.082 --> 00:46:41.002
do you think of like year three in Toronto

00:46:41.002 --> 00:46:44.892
of just like this two weeks
where you were just 30 and 20?

00:46:44.892 --> 00:46:46.592
Was there ever a period
that you actually remember

00:46:46.592 --> 00:46:49.712
being ultra hot for like
a week or two or a month?

00:46:49.712 --> 00:46:51.832
- Nothing out of the ordinary.

00:46:51.832 --> 00:46:53.762
Maybe it was a couple times in a month

00:46:53.762 --> 00:46:54.922
I feel like I couldn't miss

00:46:54.922 --> 00:46:57.732
but you forget those pretty quickly

00:46:57.732 --> 00:46:59.392
'cause it keeps coming at you.

00:46:59.392 --> 00:47:00.809
Yeah, you remember,

00:47:00.809 --> 00:47:02.412
I remember the big stuff.

00:47:02.412 --> 00:47:03.572
After awhile, I was like, all right,

00:47:03.572 --> 00:47:05.462
I was hot for a week.

00:47:05.462 --> 00:47:06.832
I still didn't get the accolades.

00:47:06.832 --> 00:47:08.642
So I didn't get those awards

00:47:08.642 --> 00:47:11.042
that normally come with hotness.

00:47:11.042 --> 00:47:12.532
So that made me kind of say aight,

00:47:12.532 --> 00:47:14.475
this really doesn't mean anything.

00:47:15.524 --> 00:47:18.302
- Talk to me about one of my
favorite cultural moments ever,

00:47:18.302 --> 00:47:20.062
that concert you guys put on

00:47:20.062 --> 00:47:20.895
when you guys all signed there.

00:47:20.895 --> 00:47:22.422
- That was pretty sweet, wasn't it?

00:47:22.422 --> 00:47:24.579
It was pretty sweet.

00:47:24.579 --> 00:47:25.782
- When's the last time you saw it

00:47:25.782 --> 00:47:28.105
like on the internet or on TV by accident?

00:47:28.105 --> 00:47:29.975
- I was watching it like the other week.

00:47:31.862 --> 00:47:32.942
Because we've been talking about it.

00:47:32.942 --> 00:47:34.244
- [Gary] It's such a fun moment, right?

00:47:34.244 --> 00:47:36.413
- Yeah, we were having fun.

00:47:36.413 --> 00:47:38.726
- You were real happy.

00:47:38.726 --> 00:47:40.462
- I remember that part the most,

00:47:40.462 --> 00:47:42.439
I'm like man he's real happy.

00:47:42.439 --> 00:47:43.712
- Oh yeah, it's warm right now.

00:47:43.712 --> 00:47:45.102
Usually it's starting to get chilly.

00:47:45.102 --> 00:47:48.692
I like the chill and the
light brisk in Toronto Falls

00:47:48.692 --> 00:47:49.865
but this is something different, man.

00:47:49.865 --> 00:47:51.152
- On some real shit,

00:47:51.152 --> 00:47:53.045
do you view that chapter of you guys,

00:47:53.902 --> 00:47:56.002
back to the four, five,
six, like it really,

00:47:56.002 --> 00:47:58.312
like I remember being like oh man,

00:47:58.312 --> 00:48:00.312
this is gonna be boring for a long time.

00:48:01.902 --> 00:48:04.902
Obviously, the Spurs had
an incredible dynasty

00:48:04.902 --> 00:48:05.882
going at the same time

00:48:05.882 --> 00:48:09.312
and that Mavericks team got
hot at the right kinda way,

00:48:09.312 --> 00:48:12.832
like I mean this is a
really challenging question

00:48:12.832 --> 00:48:14.822
and I'm not looking to
make it challenging.

00:48:14.822 --> 00:48:16.679
I'm just genuinely curious.

00:48:16.679 --> 00:48:18.252
I really am curious,

00:48:18.252 --> 00:48:21.282
do you view that chapter as a success

00:48:21.282 --> 00:48:23.042
or is there a level of disappointment

00:48:23.042 --> 00:48:24.372
because you thought you
could have done more?

00:48:24.372 --> 00:48:26.662
- No, it's success, it's success.

00:48:26.662 --> 00:48:27.622
- I can see it either way.

00:48:27.622 --> 00:48:29.331
I really genuinely didn't know,

00:48:29.331 --> 00:48:30.164
I was curious.

00:48:30.164 --> 00:48:31.432
- Well I mean people have to,

00:48:31.432 --> 00:48:33.922
so the whole story behind that was right,

00:48:33.922 --> 00:48:35.632
we signed, we're there,

00:48:35.632 --> 00:48:37.962
the decision is the night before,

00:48:37.962 --> 00:48:40.982
we're having crazy parties as Miami.

00:48:40.982 --> 00:48:44.252
I had never seen that many
cameras around our hotel.

00:48:44.252 --> 00:48:47.202
We were at the W.

00:48:47.202 --> 00:48:48.722
I mean sea of cameras,

00:48:48.722 --> 00:48:49.852
hundreds and hundreds of cameras.

00:48:49.852 --> 00:48:51.772
I've never, we haven't even played a game.

00:48:51.772 --> 00:48:53.062
So the next day,

00:48:53.062 --> 00:48:54.202
I know basketball,

00:48:54.202 --> 00:48:56.512
all right we're gonna
do the press conference,

00:48:56.512 --> 00:48:57.802
we're gonna go through the whole thing,

00:48:57.802 --> 00:48:58.982
we hold up the jerseys,

00:48:58.982 --> 00:49:00.532
boom, we gonna get out of here.

00:49:02.492 --> 00:49:04.822
12 o'clock comes noon nothing

00:49:04.822 --> 00:49:06.602
to one o'clock, two
o'clock, three o'clock,

00:49:06.602 --> 00:49:07.882
nobody's hitting us back.

00:49:07.882 --> 00:49:09.432
We're just sitting in the hotel room

00:49:09.432 --> 00:49:10.902
just sitting there.
- The three of you?

00:49:10.902 --> 00:49:13.183
- No, no, no, me and my family.

00:49:13.183 --> 00:49:14.162
I hadn't slept in 24 hours.

00:49:14.162 --> 00:49:15.852
I was just up.

00:49:15.852 --> 00:49:18.612
So they eventually call us,

00:49:18.612 --> 00:49:22.792
aight, hey come down to the
arena about five or six o'clock.

00:49:22.792 --> 00:49:23.972
We pull up to the arena,

00:49:23.972 --> 00:49:26.292
it's thousands and thousands of people.

00:49:26.292 --> 00:49:30.222
They got jerseys printed
already, posters everywhere,

00:49:30.222 --> 00:49:32.242
I mean it was overwhelming.

00:49:32.242 --> 00:49:33.942
So then we got in there and they said,

00:49:33.942 --> 00:49:35.639
all right this is what's going on

00:49:35.639 --> 00:49:37.879
and they explained everything to us

00:49:37.879 --> 00:49:39.645
and I was like yo, that's so cool.

00:49:42.362 --> 00:49:43.509
This is crazy.

00:49:43.509 --> 00:49:45.042
And they had the build up there

00:49:45.042 --> 00:49:46.922
because there was a concert
there the next day,.

00:49:46.922 --> 00:49:48.602
I just found this out.

00:49:48.602 --> 00:49:52.482
So everybody thought we like we didn't--

00:49:52.482 --> 00:49:53.952
- Honestly I wasn't
even worried about that.

00:49:53.952 --> 00:49:57.162
It was just that image in my
head of like the three of you,

00:49:57.162 --> 00:49:58.472
you guys were just like,

00:49:58.472 --> 00:50:00.328
that must have just been
an incredible moment.

00:50:00.328 --> 00:50:01.322
- I mean come on, it was amazing.

00:50:01.322 --> 00:50:04.022
It was 25,000 people
there with no basketball.

00:50:04.022 --> 00:50:08.649
- And you're in your 20s and
optimism is attractive to me.

00:50:08.649 --> 00:50:12.360
And you guys were like we're
gonna win 87 fucking times.

00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:13.722
- You saw what happened when
Jordan and them got together,

00:50:13.722 --> 00:50:15.032
what about us?

00:50:15.032 --> 00:50:15.865
It was just like,

00:50:15.865 --> 00:50:17.272
and you know we were just,

00:50:17.272 --> 00:50:20.159
it was kind of being young
and naive at that time too

00:50:20.159 --> 00:50:21.722
'cause everybody was happy.

00:50:21.722 --> 00:50:23.922
We weren't worried about
watching what we say.

00:50:23.922 --> 00:50:26.762
We didn't know that things could get out

00:50:26.762 --> 00:50:27.732
as quickly as they could.

00:50:27.732 --> 00:50:29.779
- How early did you
think about free agency

00:50:29.779 --> 00:50:31.272
at that point of your career?

00:50:31.272 --> 00:50:33.882
Like when did it start
seeping into your head?

00:50:33.882 --> 00:50:35.672
Like a year before it happened,

00:50:35.672 --> 00:50:37.665
during that season, late?

00:50:38.503 --> 00:50:39.336
- [Chris] Before 2010?

00:50:39.336 --> 00:50:42.557
- Yeah like when did, as
a kid, you're a human,

00:50:42.557 --> 00:50:44.882
you were like, I know
you were professional

00:50:44.882 --> 00:50:46.492
and focused on the season at hand,

00:50:46.492 --> 00:50:47.325
but you're fucking human.

00:50:47.325 --> 00:50:48.242
You might have thought about,

00:50:48.242 --> 00:50:50.592
I got kids that we're talking to right now

00:50:50.592 --> 00:50:52.152
for next NFL Draft

00:50:52.152 --> 00:50:54.212
who are worried about
their second contract

00:50:54.212 --> 00:50:55.742
four or five years from now

00:50:55.742 --> 00:50:58.592
and I think that's smart, not stupid.

00:50:58.592 --> 00:51:01.042
So I'm just actually curious for you

00:51:01.042 --> 00:51:03.442
when did free agency and
how that was gonna play out

00:51:03.442 --> 00:51:04.492
play out in your mind?

00:51:04.492 --> 00:51:07.102
- It kind of really start
playing into it 2008.

00:51:07.102 --> 00:51:07.935
- [Gary] Two years before.

00:51:07.935 --> 00:51:08.768
- Yeah, two years before.

00:51:08.768 --> 00:51:11.292
Not that I was obsessing,

00:51:11.292 --> 00:51:13.187
we had signed the deal,

00:51:13.187 --> 00:51:15.322
the three with the four year option.

00:51:15.322 --> 00:51:17.712
So that was getting closer

00:51:17.712 --> 00:51:19.722
and once that started getting closer,

00:51:19.722 --> 00:51:21.492
it became this thing.

00:51:21.492 --> 00:51:22.782
So people would ask you about it,

00:51:22.782 --> 00:51:24.431
they force it in your head.

00:51:24.431 --> 00:51:26.636
So they make you think about.

00:51:26.636 --> 00:51:27.969
And people lead.

00:51:30.513 --> 00:51:31.772
The people lead you and then--

00:51:31.772 --> 00:51:33.325
- [Gary] You're human.

00:51:33.325 --> 00:51:34.158
- Yeah well I was human

00:51:34.158 --> 00:51:35.922
and I didn't put two and two together

00:51:35.922 --> 00:51:39.212
as far as like having the
foresight to look at the class.

00:51:39.212 --> 00:51:41.852
Then you see who all's
gonna be available in 2010,

00:51:41.852 --> 00:51:45.512
alright yeah it's gonna
be a shit show in 2010,

00:51:45.512 --> 00:51:46.622
I got to get ready for it.

00:51:46.622 --> 00:51:49.908
But every single day it was
questions and everything

00:51:49.908 --> 00:51:51.272
but I had eventually,

00:51:51.272 --> 00:51:54.682
I had to get to the point where
I'm back to leading my team.

00:51:54.682 --> 00:51:57.522
So I couldn't really
worry about that stuff.

00:51:57.522 --> 00:51:58.582
Because they wanted me to leave,

00:51:58.582 --> 00:52:00.825
Vince, left, you're
gonna leave, aren't you?

00:52:01.802 --> 00:52:02.972
I had to deal with all that stuff.

00:52:02.972 --> 00:52:04.522
So that wasn't that fun.

00:52:04.522 --> 00:52:05.355
- No, it wasn't.

00:52:05.355 --> 00:52:06.942
It's constant story up in Toronto.

00:52:06.942 --> 00:52:08.752
- I mean it kind of sucked and

00:52:08.752 --> 00:52:12.462
I was just making a decision
to play on the big stage.

00:52:12.462 --> 00:52:13.512
I wanted to play on the big stage.

00:52:13.512 --> 00:52:15.681
- Who did you think was gonna draft you?

00:52:15.681 --> 00:52:16.514
This is always my favorite question.

00:52:16.514 --> 00:52:17.422
- People always told me Toronto.

00:52:17.422 --> 00:52:20.079
It was either Toronto or Miami, yeah.

00:52:20.079 --> 00:52:22.652
And so that's why it's kind
of funny going to Miami,

00:52:22.652 --> 00:52:23.822
it was kind of coming full circle--

00:52:23.822 --> 00:52:25.382
- Those were the two
teams that you kind of,

00:52:25.382 --> 00:52:28.460
your agent, the process,
the interviews beforehand,

00:52:28.460 --> 00:52:29.842
you felt like those were
the ones zeroing in?

00:52:29.842 --> 00:52:32.322
- Yeah, it was four, five
for me the whole time.

00:52:32.322 --> 00:52:35.112
And people were telling me

00:52:35.112 --> 00:52:36.472
yeah, you're not gonna make it to five.

00:52:36.472 --> 00:52:38.379
So just be ready,

00:52:38.379 --> 00:52:39.842
you're going to Toronto.

00:52:39.842 --> 00:52:41.272
You still don't believe 'em.

00:52:41.272 --> 00:52:42.642
You still have to go through the process

00:52:42.642 --> 00:52:44.512
of getting drafted and everything

00:52:44.512 --> 00:52:46.602
and it worked out.

00:52:46.602 --> 00:52:48.262
- What do you think about
all those baggy suits?

00:52:48.262 --> 00:52:49.512
- They were terrible.

00:52:49.512 --> 00:52:51.944
I looked at my suit, I
thought I was so cool.

00:52:51.944 --> 00:52:52.902
- [Gary] Of course that
was the fresh style then.

00:52:52.902 --> 00:52:54.429
- Yeah, that was but man.

00:52:54.429 --> 00:52:55.262
- Wait till people look back

00:52:55.262 --> 00:52:56.095
at the shit they're wearing right now.

00:52:56.095 --> 00:52:58.602
- Oh yeah yeah with the
spikes and crystals--

00:52:58.602 --> 00:53:01.578
- Beanies and tight jeans, these nerds.

00:53:01.578 --> 00:53:03.024
These kids are gonna be like,

00:53:03.024 --> 00:53:05.312
who the fuck was I 20 years from now?

00:53:05.312 --> 00:53:06.985
- [Chris] I think what
Trey Young wore shorts.

00:53:06.985 --> 00:53:08.120
- I know, I know.

00:53:08.120 --> 00:53:09.412
It's crazy to watch fashion evolve.

00:53:09.412 --> 00:53:10.832
Chris, thanks for being on the show.

00:53:10.832 --> 00:53:12.016
- [Chris] Man, I appreciate it, man.

00:53:12.016 --> 00:53:12.849
- Real pleasure.

00:53:12.849 --> 00:53:13.682
- [Chris] Thanks for having me, man.

00:53:13.682 --> 00:53:14.515
- Yeah, real pleasure.

00:53:14.515 --> 00:53:17.472
This was episode 369, was it?

00:53:17.472 --> 00:53:19.505
329, all right, we got 40
more to go to get there.

00:53:19.505 --> 00:53:23.942
212-931-5731, hit it,
thank you, thanks Chris.

00:53:23.942 --> 00:53:24.892
- [Chris] Thank you, man.

00:53:24.892 --> 00:53:26.765
- Awesome, that was fun.

